# Fleet configuration for launch_sitl.sh.
#
# The script sources ./sitl.conf when it exists (override with SITL_CONF=path),
# so this is an ordinary shell file -- no quoting needed unless a value has
# spaces. Command-line flags override anything set here, and this file in turn
# overrides an exported variable of the same name.
#
# Copy it to sitl.conf and edit the two required values.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The machine running the simulator, i.e. the Unreal editor. This is the one
# thing the image cannot carry, because it is a property of your network.
#
# On a plain Linux host there is no safe default. entrypoint.sh could only
# guess, and the only thing it could guess is the default gateway -- a router.
# Packets sent there are not rejected, they are simply never answered, so the
# failure looks like a slow simulator until the handshake times out ten
# seconds later. Rather than do that, the entrypoint refuses to guess.
#
# WSL2: leave this empty and the container falls back to its default gateway,
# which under WSL2 NAT *is* the Windows host -- but only because the script
# runs the container with --network host. Under the default bridge that
# gateway is the docker bridge, which reaches the WSL VM and not the Windows
# machine the editor is on. Anywhere other than WSL the fallback is refused.
SIM_IP=192.168.1.50

# Which aircraft to start, by VehicleIndex. This is the same number as
# UInavSimBridge.VehicleIndex on the pawn, and every port is derived from it:
# udp/(49000 + index) for the simulator link, tcp/(5760 + 10*index) for MSP.
# Nothing converts between the two sides, which is the point -- one number
# decides which aircraft a container talks to.
#
# It has to match the port table the plugin prints when the level comes up:
#   LogInavSim: INAV Sim: 4 vehicles -- 1:udp/49000 ... 4:udp/49003
# Starting an index the table does not list leaves that container talking to
# nobody; starting the same index twice puts two containers on one port.
INDICES=0,1,2,3

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL -- defaults shown, uncomment to change
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# IMAGE=inav-sitl:9.1
# IMAGE_TAR=inav-sitl-9.1.tar.gz   # loaded automatically when IMAGE is absent
# SIM_BASE_PORT=49000              # must equal InavSimSettings > BaseListenPort
# TCP_BASE_PORT=5760               # MSP; each vehicle strides by 10
# CHANMAP=M01-01,M02-02,M03-03,M04-04

# Where the RC sticks come from. Two eeproms ship in the image and they differ
# in exactly one field, receiver_type:
#
#   sim (default)  the simulator's inav_ue/rc/channel[*] datarefs, i.e. the
#                  pawn's SetRcChannel. A pilot in the editor.
#   msp            MSP_SET_RAW_RC from a client on tcp/(TCP_BASE_PORT + 10*index).
#                  An algorithm.
#
# Neither one changes the physics or the sensors: the simulator link stays up
# either way, and the aircraft is still flown in Unreal. This selects the
# source of the sticks, nothing else.
#
# Both eeproms are baked into the image, so neither choice needs a file from
# the host. If you are running docker by hand rather than through this script,
# the same switch is -e RECEIVER=msp -- or -e SIM=msp, which the entrypoint
# translates to the same thing (it cannot be passed to --sim: target.c accepts
# only rf|xp|ue, and anything else leaves the flight controller with no
# simulator link at all).
#
# Note that SIM and RECEIVER are not alternatives -- they answer different
# questions and both are needed. SIM picks the simulator *backend* (rf|xp|ue),
# i.e. which datarefs get subscribed and where the sensors and motor outputs
# go. RECEIVER picks who reads the sticks. SIM=xp RECEIVER=msp is a sensible
# pairing, which is why this cannot be one variable.
#
# If you fly it over MSP, the client has to keep sending. The MSP receiver
# times out after 200 ms of silence, which failsafes the aircraft -- 50 Hz is
# a comfortable rate. See docker/msp_rc_client.py.
# RECEIVER=msp

# Each container sends subscription packets to SIM_IP and then listens on
# udp/(SIM_BASE_PORT + index) for the replies, so the simulator's firewall
# needs an inbound rule for that range -- on Windows, 49000-49007/udp.
#
# The MSP ports are the other direction and a real bind, on the machine
# running the containers. Under --network host those are host ports, so if
# something else already holds tcp/5760, move TCP_BASE_PORT.

# Per-vehicle eeprom persistence. Unset by default: every vehicle boots from
# the configured eeprom baked into the image and keeps any changes in the
# container's own writable layer. That makes a rerun of the script a reset to
# a known-good state, and leaves nothing on the host that can break it.
#
# Set it to keep a CLI `save` across container replacement. The directory
# has to be writable by uid 1000 *inside* the container -- the script checks
# and prints the chown if it is not.
# DATA_ROOT=/srv/sitl-data

# On WSL the daemon usually needs sudo; on a server the operator is normally
# in the docker group and it does not.
# DOCKER=sudo\ docker
